    {
      "id": "f8",
      "statement": "What distinguishes the alerts: both have 6 senders and 6 transfers inside one hour, but the recorded relationships differ. (a) A-9278: all contributing credits are passed on within the gap, in two batches of three, through three hops to one resting account. Each hop takes slightly less than the one before (for example 138000 → 135000 → 132000), with gaps of 140,000–195,000 ms after the first hop. All three hops were sent from one device, D-568, by three different accounts. (b) A-9895: identical small credits; only half are linked onward, through a single hop of 14000. No device is shared on the route, and the resting account's later outflow falls outside the onward window. So the A-9278 alert is a fan-in followed by fast, layered, device-linked onward movement. The A-9895 alert is, as recorded, a fan-in with partial single-hop forwarding and no device linkage.",
